<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Max Uhle</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1856">1856</a>–<a href="/years/1944">1944</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Friedrich Max Uhle</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Max Uhle was a German archaeologist whose work in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peru">Peru</a>, Chile, Ecuador and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bolivia">Bolivia</a> at the turn of the 20th century had a significant impact on the practice of South American archaeology. Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dresden">Dresden</a> and trained as a philologist, he took his doctorate at the University of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leipzig">Leipzig</a> in 1880, and in 1892 published, with B. von Grumbkow, the first in-depth scientific account of Tiwanaku. From 1896 he excavated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pachacamac">Pachacamac</a> and Mochica and Chimu sites, recovering about 9,000 artifacts, and built a chronology from their styles that Alfred Kroeber later advanced and that remains key to the dating of pre-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/inca-empire">Inca</a> Peru. In 1917 he was the first to describe the Chinchorro mummies scientifically.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/max-uhle.jpg" alt="Max Uhle" width="244" height="310" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: public domain · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Uhle_max.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Max Uhle</b> excavated at the Huacas de Moche in 1899–1900, and Rafael Larco Hoyle, whose collection forms the Museo Larco in Lima, defined the culture and its phases.</blockquote><a href="/empire/moche">Moche · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Aspero on the coast was identified as early as 1905, the year <b>Max Uhle</b> also noted Caral without recognising its hills as pyramids.</blockquote><a href="/culture/caral-supe-civilisation">Caral–Supe civilisation · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>The term “trophy head”, coined by the archaeologist <b>Max Uhle</b>, comes from his reading of severed heads in ancient Peruvian art as trophies of war, though early Nazca pottery suggests that their first purpose may have been tied to farming.</blockquote><a href="/culture/nazca-culture">Nazca culture · Legacy</a></li></ul></section>
